  "name": "Parts of speech: noun, verb, adjective …",
  "level": "A1",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "subtitle": "Every word has a type. If you know the type, you know what the word does in the sentence: nouns name things, verbs show actions, adjectives say what something is like.",
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "lookup": { "href": "/grammar/parts-of-speech", "label": "Look up all parts of speech", "chips": "wortarten" },
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      { "text": "Noun · who? what?" },
      { "text": "Verb · what do they do?", "variant": "verb" },
      { "text": "Adjective · what is it like?", "variant": "prep" }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "The <strong>cat</strong> <span class=\"vp-verb\">sleeps</span> on the <span class=\"vp-prep\">warm</span> <strong>sofa</strong>. · Amira <span class=\"vp-verb\">plays</span> <strong>football</strong> on Sundays.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>Nouns</strong> are names for people, animals, things, places and ideas: <em>a man, the dog, a table, London, happiness</em>. They often have <em>a, an</em> or <em>the</em> in front.",
        "tag": "noun"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Verbs</strong> say what someone does or what happens: <em>I work, she sleeps, they eat</em>. After <em>he, she, it</em> the verb often ends in <strong>-s</strong>: <em>He works.</em> Not: <em>He work.</em>",
        "tag": "verb"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Adjectives</strong> say what something is like: <em>a big house, The house is big.</em> They come <strong>before a noun</strong> or after <em>be</em>, and they <strong>never</strong> take <em>-s</em>: <em>two big houses</em>, not <em>two bigs houses</em>.",
        "tag": "adjective"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Adverbs</strong> say how, when or where: <em>quickly, today, here, often</em>. Many adverbs end in <strong>-ly</strong>: <em>She walks slowly.</em>",
        "tag": "adverb"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Pronouns</strong> take the place of a noun: <em>Tom is nice. <strong>He</strong> helps me.</em> <strong>Prepositions</strong> show place or time: <em>in, on, at, with</em>. <strong>Conjunctions</strong> join words and sentences: <em>and, but, or, because</em>. <strong>Articles</strong> are <em>a, an, the</em>.",
        "tag": "small words"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Some words have two jobs. <em>I <strong>drink</strong> tea</em> (verb) and <em>I want a <strong>drink</strong></em> (noun). The sentence tells you which one it is. More about nouns: <a href=\"/nomen\">Nouns: people, things, places</a>. All parts of speech with examples: <a href=\"/grammar/wortarten\">Look up parts of speech</a>.",
    "reference": {
